## (3) New defects in v2

**N1 — P1 — `run-fuel`'s bounded-return contract fails through `ffi/maybe-run`.** Plan §3.4 defines `run-fuel` as `(ffi/maybe-run machine (fn [m] …))` and guarantees (§3.4, honest-guarantee paragraph) interruption "after at most `n` instructions of each task of its tree per program tick." But `maybe-run` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:334-350`) loops — `(recur (run-fn vm))` after every answered bridge step — with no budget, iteration cap, or return to the caller. A bridge-served program that keeps making FFI requests (each iteration individually fuel-bounded, the loop not) never returns from one `run-fuel` call, so the holder never regains control: exactly the unbounded-in-one-tick class F3 required closing, one seam over. The plan's bullet "each loop costs another slice" concedes the iterations exist but never bounds them per call. Minimal fix: give `run-fuel` a bounded bridge loop — e.g. return the machine after one bridge cycle and let the holder re-invoke, or thread a call-level slice budget into a `maybe-run` variant — and add an acceptance case (an FFI-looping program preempted by `run-fuel`) to 6d-a item 9.

**N2 — REFUTED as a code defect; the plan's mechanism is right and one of its sentences is wrong (P3 documentation).** My candidate was that the first-wins census (`ucf/rows.cljc:1388-1393`, `cell!` registers only the first body per cell id) would drop a `:ready` row's successor cell. Reading the lift order refutes it: a ready entry's pending registers its cell via `cell-for!` (`:1542-1544`), which reads the machine's own `:resources` — and `check-wait-set` (`engine.cljc:2192-2199`) has already advanced those resources to the final successor before any entry is queued, so every registration of one cell id in one lift reads the identical current body and first-wins aliasing is consistent; carried values, not re-reads, deliver the intermediate elements, in queue order. What is wrong is v2 §3.2's sentence claiming "the task's `:cursor-cells` row holds the current, the ready row the successor; two positions of one cell id" — there are not two positions; there is one canonical (final) body, and that is precisely why it works. Minimal fix: amend that sentence in §3.2 to say the ready row references the census's single current cell body, and pin the semantics with a shared-cursor case (two waiters on one cursor, snapshot after the sweep before any resume) in 6d-a acceptance item 3. No code change needed.

**N3 — P1 — The kernel gate guard is sequenced one slice too late.** Plan §3.2 gives 6d-a the restore: a `:running` task restores with `:control` set, and "a fenced restore puts the ready list in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`… and leaves `:ready-queue []`" — but nothing strips or parks the control. The existing fenced arm (`ucf/rows.cljc:2109-2111`) moves only waits and parked records into recovery. The `vm-hot` entry guard that makes "the exporting machine is pure data" true is assigned to 6d-b (§3.3 outcome 4; §3.9 6d-b item 3). So throughout 6d-a, `lower-task {:fenced true}` on a running root yields a machine with control set and gate `:exporting` that `vm-hot` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:137-143`, no gate check) will happily step — pure computation advances, windows mutate, and rehydrate byte-equality is destroyed. The second review explicitly required kernel fencing to ship with the first exposed running path. Minimal fix: move the one-check `vm-hot` entry guard from 6d-b item 3 into 6d-a (it is a single `contains?` read at entry, outside the instruction loop), and add the "fenced running machine refuses `vm/step`" assertion to 6d-a acceptance instead of only 6d-b.

**N4 — P2 — One §3.6 coverage class is unreachable by the driver §3.6 defines.** §3.6 requires the stepping driver to pin coverage by structural class including "scheduler product (… blocked + ready)" and asserts "each class is reached at least once on the corpus"; §3.9 item 4 defines that driver as `run-fuel 1` boundaries only. But `run-fuel` on a blocked machine performs the entire budgeted round — sweep, budgeted installs, and the resume (§3.4) — so the post-sweep/pre-resume blocked+ready state never materializes as a returned boundary. Item 3(d) in fact constructs that state by calling `check-wait-set` directly, outside the driver. As written, the coverage assertion in §3.6/§5.7 cannot pass. Minimal fix: either pin blocked+ready solely through the products test (items 3d/6) and strike it from the driver's class list, or give the harness an explicit round-phase seam and say so in §3.6. (The companion re-examination — halted-beside-queue reclassified `:blocked` in §3.1 — I checked and it is CORRECT, not a defect: `run-loop` (`engine.cljc:2216-2219`) reads the queue before the halt, a terminal ready entry throws in `resume-from-run-queue` (`:2310-2311`) before any halt value is read, and any resumed activation overwrites the halt when it next parks or halts; the halt value is dead by the kernel's own order.)

**N5 — P3 — The completed-`:put` op-id well-formedness check has no site.** §3.1's `task-ops` bullet says a ready `:put`'s op-id "is checked well-formed (`check-op-id!`) but excluded from `:yin.k/ops`". In the current code `check-op-id!` is called from inside `task-ops` itself (`ucf/rows.cljc:1286-1291`); excluding ready rows from `task-ops` removes the only caller, and no other stage in §3.1's validator table is named as performing the check (acceptance item 2 tests row shape and status-keyword, not op-id form). A malformed op-id inside a carried ready `:put` pending would therefore pass lower silently — harmless to the receiver (it delivers `:V`, never re-appends), but the plan's stated check does not exist as specified. Minimal fix: name the site — e.g. `check-pendings!` over slot 18 reuses `check-op-id!` for form only — and add one malformed-op-id ready-row refusal to acceptance item 2.

## (4) The eight §3.10 defaults

(As I treat them: 1. task slots 17 `:run` / 18 `:ready` with a one-time re-mint after module-pins; 2. fuel as holder config `:fuel`, default nil; 3. no automatic export at the bound in 6d-b, explicit `hand-off` only; 4. no adoption of a retained boundary, recovery is the last durable checkpoint; 5. keep edge-reachability liveness (Q6) and pin the pre-branch `ghost` false refusal; 6. completed `:put` wakes are completed operations, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`; 7. `:push` relabelled to its successor, not refused; 8. holds stay refusals as documented driver transients.)

1. **SAFE** — separate slots remove F7's silent-misread class by construction (every slot-13 traversal stays wait-only), and the forced re-mint coincides with module-pins' re-mint that has already landed on this tree.
2. **SAFE** — fuel as an opt-in composition knob keeps clocks and time-conversion out of the VM while making preemption available; the invariant closes only for compositions that set it, and the plan says so rather than hiding it (subject to N1's bound actually holding).
3. **SAFE** — `hand-off` is already the composition's explicit act in the driver, `:yin.k/at-boundary` supplies the signal, and a later tenure-aware auto-arm policy needs no carrier or VM change.
4. **SAFE** — an ended holder holds no tenure, so adoption would be new ledger authority; retaining the boundary for inspection only, with the checkpoint-loss sentence written into the documents, is the honest specified outcome F4 demanded.
5. **SAFE** — the `ghost` refusal is a bounded, documented discharge conservatism (`:yin.k/unsatisfied` at a pc whose segment cannot be discharged), not a scheduler-state incompleteness; removing it is a separate constant-propagation slice and should not gate 6d-a.
6. **SAFE** — the `:put` already appended under the source's own authority before the snapshot; counting it as a retained write would make the receiver re-append a datom already on the stream, duplicating the effect.
7. **SAFE** — `debruijn/stack.cljc:722-723` shows `:push` only advances pc with the value already on the stack, so relabelling to the successor runs no guest semantics and loses nothing.
8. **SAFE** — holds are driver-internal observations mid-step, not task states at rest, and the default as stated carries its own verification duty (6d-b proves each resolves within one cycle without guest execution, or files a known-gaps row).

