- **O2 — The two P1 fixes folded into 6d-a's scope:** the bounded bridge loop for `run-fuel` (N1, with its FFI-loop acceptance case) and the `vm-hot` gate guard moved from 6d-b (N3, with the fenced-running-step refusal pinned in 6d-a). Without O2, 6d-a ships a preemption API whose bound is false for bridge-served machines and a fenced running machine that can be stepped.
- **O3 — A decision on the blocked+ready harness seam** (N4): strike the class from the §3.6 driver coverage list (pinning it via items 3d/6 only) or specify the round-phase seam the harness will use; the acceptance text must not assert a class its own driver cannot reach.
- **O4 — The shared-cursor ready case and the §3.2 sentence correction** (N2): two waiters on one cursor, snapshot after the sweep before any resume, law 1 + law 2 — this is the one ready-carrier aliasing case the F1/F2 acceptance cases (distinct callers/streams) do not exercise.
- **O5 — A named op-id form check for ready `:put` pendings** (N5) with one malformed-row refusal test, so the §3.1 claim is implemented where the brief says it is.

Summary of the seat's position: v2 answers eight of the nine findings completely and F3 in all but one seam; the grammar, product state model, validator, custody, expiry, stack-layout, and harness-order work is sound and well-evidenced (N2 and the halted-beside-queue rule were checked and are correct). It is NOT-READY as literally specified because N1 leaves `run-fuel`'s central bound false and N3 leaves a steppable exporting machine exposed for the whole of 6d-a — both are one-paragraph amendments to §3.4/§3.9 plus one moved guard, after which this seat would return IMPLEMENTATION-READY without further design review.
